; The declarations another module is warned about for using (`D-122`). They are
; in a module of their own on purpose: a `deprecated` is interface, so the
; manager has to rebuild what depends on this file when one is added or taken
; away, and a warning that only ever appeared in the module it was written in
; would prove nothing about that.
(export widen retry-limit Reading)

(fn (deprecated "call `widen-checked` instead") widen ((value i32)) -> i64
  (as i64 value))

(const (deprecated "read the limit from the manifest") retry-limit 3)

; A field carries the slot too, and it is the migration that actually happens:
; a record is fine and one field of it is going away (`D-152`). Deprecating the
; whole `struct` would warn at every mention of a type that is mostly healthy,
; and the warning would stop being read.
(struct Reading
  (((deprecated "read `celsius`") degrees i64) (celsius i64) (label String)))
